WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.440
When I look back on my childhood, or I remember some words from Spanish or from Russian,

2
00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:15.439
or I bring to mind one of Maxwell's equations, I'm drawing on portions of my brain involved

3
00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:21.680
in long-term memory. But when I'm trying to hold a few ideas in mind to connect them together so I

4
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:28.719
can understand a concept or solve a problem, I'm using my working memory. Obviously, sometimes

5
00:00:28.719 --> 00:00:33.759
I'll bring something from my long-term memory into my working memory so I can think about it.

6
00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:40.159
So the two types of memory are related. There are lots of different ways to slice our understanding

7
00:00:40.159 --> 00:00:45.200
of memory, but for this course on learning, we're going to talk about only these two

8
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:51.680
major memory systems, working memory and long-term memory. Working memory is the

9
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.959
part of memory that has to do with what you're immediately and consciously processing in your

10
00:00:56.959 --> 00:01:02.720
mind. Your working memory is centered out of the prefrontal cortex, although as we'll see later,

11
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.760
there are also connections to other parts of your brain so you can access long-term memories.

12
00:01:08.559 --> 00:01:15.279
Researchers used to think that our working memory could hold around seven items or chunks, but now

13
00:01:15.279 --> 00:01:22.080
it's widely believed that the working memory holds only about four chunks of information. We tend to

14
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.760
automatically group memory items into chunks so it seems our working memory is bigger than it

15
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:35.519
actually is. Although your working memory is like a blackboard, it's not a very good blackboard. You

16
00:01:35.519 --> 00:01:40.639
often need to keep repeating what you're trying to work with so it stays in your working memory.

17
00:01:41.199 --> 00:01:46.319
For example, you'll sometimes repeat a phone number to yourself until you have a chance to

18
00:01:46.319 --> 00:01:54.000
write it down. Repetitions needed so that your metabolic vampires, that is natural dissipating

19
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.680
processes, don't suck those memories away. You may find yourself shutting your eyes to keep any other

20
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:07.279
items from intruding into the limited slots of your working memory as you concentrate.

21
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:15.360
So we know that short-term memory is something like an inefficient mental blackboard. The other

22
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:22.479
form of memory, long-term memory, is like a storage warehouse. And just like a warehouse,

23
00:02:22.479 --> 00:02:29.759
it's distributed over a big area. Different kinds of long-term memories are stored in different

24
00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:36.080
regions of the brain. Research has shown that when you first try to put an item of information

25
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:42.240
in long-term memory, you need to revisit it at least a few times to increase the chances that

26
00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:48.160
you'll be able to find it later when you might need it. The long-term memory storage warehouse

27
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:55.199
is immense. It's got room for billions of items. In fact, there can be so many items they can bury

28
00:02:55.199 --> 00:03:01.279
each other. So it can be difficult for you to find the information you need unless you practice and

29
00:03:01.279 --> 00:03:08.720
repeat at least a few times. Long-term memory is important because it's where you store fundamental

30
00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.639
concepts and techniques that are often involved in whatever you're learning about.

31
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:20.000
When you encounter something new, you often use your working memory to handle it. If you want to

32
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.199
move that information into your long-term memory, it often takes time and practice. To help with this

33
00:03:27.199 --> 00:03:34.559
process, use a technique called spaced repetition. This technique involves repeating what you're

34
00:03:34.559 --> 00:03:41.600
trying to retain. But what you want to do is space this repetition out, repeating a new vocabulary

35
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:49.360
word or a problem-solving technique, for example, over a number of days. Extending your practice over

36
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.479
several days does make a difference. Research has shown that if you try to glue things into your

37
00:03:54.479 --> 00:04:01.119
memory by repeating something 20 times in one evening, for example, it won't stick nearly as

38
00:04:01.119 --> 00:04:07.279
well as if you practice it the same number of times over several days. This is like building

39
00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:14.080
the brick wall we saw earlier. If you don't leave time for the mortar to dry, that is, time for the

40
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:21.279
synoptic connections to form and strengthen, you won't have a very good structure. And talk about

41
00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:28.399
lasting structure. Look at this part of the Acropolis here. Thanks for learning about learning.

42
00:04:28.399 --> 00:04:38.640
I'm Barbara Oakley.